  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 5 (score 179.9, the best raced design so far): the 7-sensor array at x = ±0.06 m with weights [-1, -0.75, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 0.75, 1] removed line loss on the oval (0 events, down from about 1 per run) and gave 2 of 20 oval finishes, matching iteration 1's finish count with better progress (0.75 vs 0.68). Steering oscillation was 0.33, below the 0.4 trigger, so kd does not need trimming. Remaining weaknesses: max_error still sits at about 0.06 m on every track, the s-bend has no finishes, and the slalom still loses the line once per run. The Evaluator's first branch applies: the finish losses in iteration 4 came from the reduced inner weights, and the wider array is kept. This iteration tests base_speed 1.3 alone, with geometry, weights, kp 5.0, kd 0.8, ki 0.02 and line_loss unchanged. Expected result: a higher oval completion rate or at least the same finishes, a shorter completion_time (the time term is worth up to 60 points), and a similar max_error. Risk: higher speed may push the robot off the tape on the sharper bends, since speed earlier changed off-track behaviour, so watch progress on the s-bend and slalom and the oval finish count. Hypotheses: (a) the robot holds the line at 1.3 with the current weights, so completion_time falls and the score rises above 180; (b) max_error stays near 0.06 m, which would point to sensor lag ahead of the axle or to the motor mismatch rather than speed. If finishes fall to 0 while line_loss stays 0, the next step is to return to base 1.2 and test the sensor row at y 0.01 m in a geometry-only race.",
